{% set local_problem %}
<p>Decatur's relationship with water makes downspouts more critical here than almost anywhere else we serve. Tennessee River wraps around your city. Wheeler Lake sits east. Water table throughout Decatur is naturally elevated — often just 3-4 feet below foundations during wet seasons. Your home is already working against groundwater pressure. The last thing it needs is roof runoff adding to the load.</p>

<p>Many <a href="/recent-projects/">Decatur homes</a> have downspout problems they don't even recognize. Builder-grade systems dump water inches from the foundation. Basic splash blocks move it 18 inches at best. In most places, that might be acceptable. In Decatur, it's asking for trouble.</p>

<p>Here's what happens: heavy rain hits, your roof sheds thousands of gallons, that water flows through gutters to downspouts, and exits — right next to your foundation. On Decatur's clay-heavy soil, that water doesn't absorb. It pools. And because groundwater is already high, there's nowhere for it to go except sideways — toward your basement or crawlspace.</p>

<p>During TVA water releases following regional rainfall, the problem intensifies. River rises, water table rises with it, and foundation soil throughout Decatur becomes saturated. <a href="/recent-projects/">Homes near Point Mallard, along Highway 31, in low-lying areas of Southwest Decatur</a> — all particularly vulnerable. Proper downspouts with adequate extensions are your first line of defense against the water you can actually control.</p>

<p><strong>Our opinion:</strong> Before you spend $2,000+ on new gutters because of overflow, let us look at your downspouts. Half the overflow problems we see in Decatur are downspout issues — not enough of them, wrong size, bad positioning. That's a much cheaper fix than replacing the entire system.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>A good downspout system in Decatur needs three things: enough capacity, proper positioning, and — most critically — effective dispersal far from your foundation.</p>

<p><strong>Capacity:</strong> One downspout for every 35-40 linear feet of gutter, maximum. And they should be 3x4 inch, not 2x3. The larger size handles roughly twice the flow rate. During intense storms, that difference determines whether water moves away from your foundation or backs up and overflows right next to it.</p>

<p><strong>Positioning:</strong> Downspouts go where water naturally collects — typically corners and low points in gutter runs. Bad positioning creates dead zones where water pools instead of draining. In Decatur's humidity, standing water in gutters accelerates corrosion.</p>

<p><strong>Dispersal:</strong> This is where Decatur homes need extra attention. Extensions should move water at least 6-10 feet from your foundation. For many homes — especially near the river or in low-lying areas — underground drains routing water 20+ feet away provide the protection your foundation actually needs.</p>

<p><strong>Warning from experience:</strong> Those corrugated black flex pipes people bury in their yards? They clog within 2-3 years. Every time. The corrugations trap debris, roots grow into them, and water stops flowing. If you want buried drainage, use smooth PVC. We dig up failed flex pipe installations constantly in Decatur — homeowners thought they solved the problem, but they created a new one.</p>
{% endset %}

